{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Implementation pushed for review: https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/2044 (commit 4819296c31). Plan-review PASSED (advisories only) and ticket claimed. Fix: _cascade_inbound_status_parents in apply_inbound_events.py shares the interactive cascade DECISION (_CASCADING_EDGES + _resolve_parent_in_status from rebar._commands.transition) and writes eligible ancestors' STATUS events first (topmost first, cycle-guarded) on both inbound-update and inbound-create paths; same-status inbound writes are now suppressed no-ops (batch self-consistency + idempotency). Deliberately NOT routed through transition_compute (would impose the LLM start-work gate + eager per-event push on reconcile). docs/concurrency.md §I4a now scopes the invariant across ALL write surfaces, citing the operator ruling. RED demonstrated: 4 affirmative tests failed pre-fix; 7/7 pass post-fix. Gates: make lint, make typecheck (624 files), complexity ratchet (new=0 increased=0), full tests/unit/rebar_reconciler suite (3040 passed). Fork check: the 'Jira holds parent closed + child active' case is NOT a new fork — the ruling makes the local invariant authoritative, and the outbound differ pushes the cascaded parent reactivation back to Jira on the next pass, exactly as after an interactive reactivation."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787361722994945001,"uuid":"33844cea-32e3-4dd1-a856-0a172c4453b3"}